"Temperature and Heat: teaching notes",
"Temperature and Heat",
"Intro physics (temperature, heat, expansion, conduction)",
"Temperature is what two bodies share once heat stops flowing between them (the zeroth law). Heat is energy in transit because of a temperature difference; a body holds internal energy, not heat.",
"In an insulated box the heat one body loses is the heat the other gains, so the final temperature is the heat capacity weighted average, (C_A T_A + C_B T_B)/(C_A + C_B). The approach is exponential, set by the bar's conductance and both heat capacities.",
"A constant volume gas thermometer is linear in temperature, and every gas sample's line points to the same zero of pressure, −{#} °C. That is why the kelvin scale starts there.",
"Solids grow by ΔL = αL₀ΔT. Liquids grow by about ten to a hundred times more per degree, and water does the odd thing: it is densest at {#} °C.",
"Steady conduction through layers adds resistances d/k, like resistors in series. The temperature drops most across the layer with the largest resistance, and the air films on each side (convection) count as layers too.",
"Students should know Q = mcΔT and the Celsius and Kelvin scales. The demo has five views: <strong>Equilibrium</strong> (two bodies joined by a {#} cm diameter conducting bar in a perfectly insulated box), <strong>Thermometer</strong> (a {#} L bulb of gas and a pressure gauge in a choice of baths), <strong>Expansion</strong> (a {#} m rod against a dial gauge, or {#} mL of liquid in a Pyrex flask with a {#} mm capillary) <strong>Conduction</strong> (steady heat flow through up to three layers) and <strong>Cooling</strong> (a metal sphere in room air losing heat by convection and radiation, with or without a fan). All the material constants are from OpenStax University Physics Volume {#}, Tables {#}, {#} and {#}. The speed buttons run the slow views faster than real time.",
"<strong>Hot metal, cool water.</strong> {#} kg of aluminum at {#} °C, {#} kg of water at {#} °C, a {#} cm copper bar. Ask Q1, then start. The two traces meet at {#} °C after a few time constants (τ = {#} s); by then <strong>Heat that has left A</strong> reads {#} kJ.",
"<strong>Mystery block.</strong> Ask Q2. The block's specific heat is hidden; students time the run to equilibrium and work it out from the final temperature.",
"<strong>Gas thermometer.</strong> Ask Q3. Put the bulb in <strong>Ice water</strong>, <strong>Boiling water</strong>, <strong>Dry ice</strong> and <strong>Liquid nitrogen</strong> in turn; each reading takes about a minute to settle (the bulb follows the bath with a {#} s time constant). Change <strong>Gas in the bulb</strong> and repeat: a different slope, the same zero.",
"<strong>Rod in a water bath</strong>, then <strong>Water near {#} °C.</strong> Ask Q4 first. The rod's gauge moves {#} mm per degree; the water's level falls as it cools, turns near {#} °C and rises again.",
"<strong>House wall in winter.</strong> Ask Q5. {#} mm plasterboard, {#} mm glass wool and {#} mm brick between {#} °C still air and −{#} °C wind: {#} W through each square meter, and {#} °C of the {#} °C drop is across the glass wool. Drag <strong>Probe position</strong> through the wall. Then switch to <strong>Window</strong> (Q6) and <strong>Rods in series</strong>.",
"<strong>Energy chunks.</strong> Load <strong>Energy chunks</strong>: the same hot aluminum and cool water with each body's thermal energy drawn as dots of one size (counted from {#} °C). Start it: dots leave A, pass through the bar and pile up in B, and the total never changes. At equilibrium the water holds more dots per kilogram than the aluminum, so thermal energy is not temperature.",
"<strong>Energy chain.</strong> Load <strong>Energy chain: bike to light</strong>. The cyclist does {#} W of work at the pedals from {#} W of food energy ({#}%), the generator passes on {#} W of electricity, the bulb makes {#} W of light: {#}% of the food energy, the rest wasted as heat at each station. Swap the bulb for the <strong>Water heater</strong> ({#} W warms {#} kg of water {#} °C a minute) or the cyclist for the Sun on a solar cell. Ask why sunlight cannot turn the generator directly.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> Predict the final temperature in <strong>Hot metal, cool water</strong>: {#} kg of aluminum (c = {#} J/(kg·°C)) at {#} °C and {#} kg of water (c = {#} J/(kg·°C)) at {#} °C. Which body's temperature changes more, and why?",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> In <strong>Mystery block</strong>, {#} kg of an unknown metal at {#} °C and {#} kg of water at {#} °C end at {#} °C. Find the metal's specific heat and use Table {#} to name it.",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> The bulb holds {#} mmol of gas in {#} L. Predict the gauge reading in ice water and in boiling water ({#} °C). Extend the straight line through those two points to zero pressure. Where does it cross, and does the answer depend on how much gas is in the bulb?",
"<strong>Q4.</strong> Predict how much a {#} m aluminum rod (α = {#} × {#}⁻⁶ /°C) grows from {#} °C to {#} °C. Then predict what the level in the capillary does as water in the flask is cooled from {#} °C to {#} °C.",
"<strong>Q5.</strong> For the house wall, the resistances per square meter are {#} (inside air), {#}/{#}, {#}/{#}, {#}/{#} and {#} (outside air), in m²·°C/W. Find the heat flow per square meter and the temperature drop across the glass wool. How much more heat would flow without the glass wool?",
"<strong>Q6.</strong> A single pane of glass {#} mm thick (k = {#} W/(m·°C)) separates {#} °C still air from a −{#} °C wind. Why does the glass itself contribute almost nothing to the resistance, and what does set the heat flow? Why does the inside of the glass feel so cold?",
"<strong>Q7.</strong> In <strong>Rods in series</strong>, a steel rod and an aluminum rod, each {#} cm long and {#} cm across, join a {#} °C bath to a {#} °C bath. Find the junction temperature and the heat flow. Which rod has the steeper temperature gradient?",
"<strong>\"Heat and temperature are the same thing.\"</strong> A bathtub of warm water holds far more internal energy than a red hot nail, but the nail has the higher temperature, and heat would flow from the nail into the water.",
"<strong>\"Metal is colder than wood at the same temperature.\"</strong> Both are at room temperature; metal conducts heat out of your hand faster, so it feels colder.",
"<strong>\"Final temperature is halfway between the two.\"</strong> Only for equal heat capacities. Water's large c pulls the final temperature toward its own.",
"<strong>\"A hole in a plate shrinks when the plate is heated.\"</strong> Every length grows by the same fraction, including the hole.",
"<strong>\"Insulation stops heat.\"</strong> It slows it. The steady flow depends on the total resistance, and in a single pane window the air films, not the glass, do almost all the work.",
